        यदि पाठाक्षरसूचकाङ्क न्यूनम् आरम्भाङ्क आदि
            संख्यानम् भवति ० ।
            ॰ ॥ AND ITS START MUST NOT BE PAST THE STORE ॥ An empty literal
            ॰ appended nothing, so `आरम्भाङ्क` is still `सूचकाङ्क योगः १` — ONE
            ॰ PAST the end. Every reader slices `पाठाक्षरकोश` by `start..start+count`,
            ॰ and `get(n+1 .. n+1)` on a store of length `n` is NOT an empty slice,
            ॰ it is `None`: the range is empty but its START is out of bounds.
            ॰
            ॰ MEASURED: `shrinkhala.t1` has FIVE empty literals and refused in a
            ॰ FRESH interpreter — "instruction 309 of block 74 names octets 1..1 and
            ॰ पाठाक्षरकोश holds 0". It passed only after other programs in the same
            ॰ interpreter had left the ARENA longer than this program's CURSOR, which
            ॰ is order dependence and not correctness. A parallel census found it;
            ॰ 24 empty literals across 8 sources were all riding on it.
            ॰
            ॰ THE START BECOMES THE CURSOR. `start == सूचकाङ्क` is in bounds for
            ॰ every store length including ०, and a zero-length run needs no octet
            ॰ to point at. Non-empty literals are untouched: a positive count means
            ॰ the append advanced the cursor, so their start is already within it.
            ॰
            ॰ THE THREE READERS STAY AS THEY ARE and become correct by this —
            ॰ `paradigm_encode.rs:1288`, `t1_exec_riscv.rs:700` and
            ॰ `pradarshana/src/pathana.rs:565`, one of them product. Fixing it here is
            ॰ one line in one place instead of three hand-written twins.
            आरम्भाङ्क भवति पाठाक्षरसूचकाङ्क ।
