        ॰ ══════ A STORE TO A MODULE-LEVEL GLOBAL — `W-283`, `research/28` ══════
        ॰ THE RULED STORAGE MODEL, built from the two kinds it rules: form the
        ॰ address as a VALUE (`वैश्विकस्थान` २१) and then store AT it
        ॰ (`स्थाननिधान` २३). A write stops being a separate design from a read.
        ॰
        ॰ THE FIVE GUARDS ARE THE GLOBAL READ'S, NOT NEW ONES, and they are
        ॰ copied deliberately rather than reinvented: the symbol exists, its KIND
        ॰ is a `चरः` declaration, it is DECLARED IN THIS SOURCE, it has a type,
        ॰ and that type is a scalar integer. Each one is read from the arena the
        ॰ read arm reads (`ir.t1:1484-1527`).
        ॰
        ॰ "DECLARED IN THIS SOURCE" IS NOT TIDINESS AND IT IS THE ONE A STORE
        ॰ WOULD BE TEMPTED TO DROP. A cross-module global's storage is laid by
        ॰ the DECLARING module, whose object is linked in only if something else
        ॰ pulled it in — so referencing its label emits a name the image may not
        ॰ contain. That margin records the measurement: lowering those on the
        ॰ READ side took the corpus from 15 assembled to 2, with the linker
        ॰ naming `सङ्केतनक्षेत्रारम्भः` and its like UNRESOLVED. A store has the
        ॰ identical exposure and no reason to be luckier.
        ॰
        ॰ ONLY A SCALAR, for the read's reason exactly: an arena global's value
        ॰ is an ADDRESS, not the word at one, so `वैश्विकस्थान` of it would form
        ॰ the address OF the pointer and the store would overwrite the pointer
        ॰ rather than the element. Those keep their stub under cause २४.
        ॰
        ॰ THE VALUE IS ALREADY BUILT above as `सममूल्यम्`, before any of this —
        ॰ the right side's calls are real whether or not the left side lowers,
        ॰ which is what the arm's own margin says and why the order is not free.
        यदि लक्ष्यभेदः समम् वास्तुॱनामाभिव्यञ्जकभेद आदि
            यदि लक्ष्यसंज्ञा अधिकम् ० आदि
                चरः लक्ष्यनामभेदः ॱॱ न६४ भवति ० ।
                यदि लक्ष्यसंज्ञा न्यूनम् अर्थॱसंज्ञाभेदकोश ॱ दैर्घ्य आदि
                    लक्ष्यनामभेदः भवति अर्थॱसंज्ञाभेदकोश अङ्कः लक्ष्यसंज्ञा अन्तः ।
                    ॰ A slot inside the length can still be `शून्यम्` — `अर्थ` has
                    ॰ two minters and only one fills all three symbol-keyed
                    ॰ arenas. The length guard does not catch a hole.
                    यदि लक्ष्यनामभेदः समम् शून्यम् आदि लक्ष्यनामभेदः भवति ० । इति
                इति
                यदि लक्ष्यनामभेदः समम् व्याकरॱचरघोषणाभेद आदि
                    ॰ THIS GUARD IS `अधिकम् ०` WHERE THE READ ARM'S IS
                    ॰ `समम् ० → refuse`, AND THE TWO ARE EQUIVALENT ONLY BECAUSE
                    ॰ THE ARENA CANNOT HOLD A NEGATIVE. Both slots are `न६४`,
                    ॰ which is SIGNED, so the polarity is not free — on a
                    ॰ negative the read arm would lower (−१ ≠ ०) and this arm
                    ॰ would refuse (−१ is not > ०). Established from the
                    ॰ WRITERS, not from the type:
                    ॰
                    ॰   artha.t1:2630  writes ० .
                    ॰   artha.t1:1792  writes `घोष्यमाणघोषणा`, whose only
                    ॰                  non-zero source is `क्रमः` at `:1908`.
                    ॰   artha.t1:1900  `चरः क्रमः ॱॱ अ६४ भवति १ ।` — UNSIGNED,
                    ॰                  starts at १, and its loop only increments.
                    ॰
                    ॰ So the arena holds ० or a positive declaration index, never
                    ॰ a negative, and the two arms agree on every value that can
                    ॰ occur. Said here because a later reader comparing the arms
                    ॰ will see two different tests and has no other way to learn
                    ॰ they are the same test.
                    ॰
                    ॰ AND THE READ ARM'S MARGIN AT `:1495` SAYS THIS ARENA "HAS
                    ॰ ONE WRITER". IT HAS TWO — `artha.t1:1792` and `:2630` —
                    ॰ plus a whole-arena reset at `:1609`. The conclusion above
                    ॰ survives the miscount because BOTH writers are
                    ॰ non-negative, but the premise is wrong, and anyone
                    ॰ reasoning about this arena from "one writer" is reasoning
                    ॰ from a count that was true when it was written.
