        ॰ ══════ `W-284` — A WRITE TO A FIELD OR AN ELEMENT ══════
        ॰ Form the address, then store AT it. The same two instructions the
        ॰ global store `W-283` emits, and the same shape as every read: an
        ॰ address is a VALUE, and a write stops being a separate design.
        ॰
        ॰ `स्थानमात्रम्` MAKES THE TARGET'S OWN ARM EMIT AN ADDRESS instead of a
        ॰ load. Nothing is re-derived here — not the field offset, not the
        ॰ element width, not one of the seventeen refusals the field chain
        ॰ raises. If the target refuses, the arm answers `मूल्याङ्कनम् ०` with a
        ॰ stub already counted under its own cause, and this arm falls through
        ॰ to the residue below exactly as it did before.
        ॰
        ॰ THE REFUSAL IS DETECTED BY THE VALUE, NOT BY A FLAG, and that is the
        ॰ one thing here that could be subtler than it looks: `अभिव्यञ्जकरचना`
        ॰ answers `मूल्याङ्कनम् ०` for every refused shape, and ० is not a live
        ॰ value id — `नवमूल्यम्` never mints one. So a zero answer is
        ॰ unambiguous, the way `क्षेत्रक्रमगणनम्`'s index-plus-one is.
        ॰
        ॰ THE VALUE IS ALREADY BUILT as `सममूल्यम्`, above, before any of this.
        ॰ The right side's instructions are real whether or not the left side
        ॰ lowers, which is what the global-store arm's margin says and why the
        ॰ order is not free.
